Free the worker threads of a context before the dictionaries they read.

ZSTD_freeCCtxContent() calls ZSTD_clearAllDicts() and then ZSTDMT_freeCCtx().
With nbWorkers >= 1, ZSTD_compressStream2() posts a job to a worker thread and
returns. The first job of a frame reads the dictionary of the context through
a pointer that it does not own (job->cdict, the localDict of the CCtx). A job
stops only in ZSTDMT_freeCCtx(), which joins the threads (POOL_free).

So ZSTD_freeCCtx() of a context that has a dictionary and a running job frees
the dictionary under that job: heap-use-after-free in ZSTDMT_compressionJob().
destroy() and close() of a node:zlib zstd stream make that call.

ZSTDMT_freeCCtx() has this order for its own dictionary. It joins the threads
and then frees mtctx->cdictLocal.

Not covered: ZSTD_CCtx_reset() with parameters, ZSTD_CCtx_loadDictionary(),
ZSTD_CCtx_refCDict() and ZSTD_CCtx_refPrefix() on a context whose jobs still
run. They free the dictionaries too. Bun does not call them on such a context.

facebook/zstd has the same order on its dev branch (01b7154f11), and no issue
there reports it (checked 2026-10-01).

--- a/lib/compress/zstd_compress.c
+++ b/lib/compress/zstd_compress.c
@@ -171,10 +171,11 @@
 {
     assert(cctx != NULL);
     assert(cctx->staticSize == 0);
-    ZSTD_clearAllDicts(cctx);
 #ifdef ZSTD_MULTITHREAD
     ZSTDMT_freeCCtx(cctx->mtctx); cctx->mtctx = NULL;
 #endif
+    /* Bun: after the join above. A job that still runs reads these dictionaries. */
+    ZSTD_clearAllDicts(cctx);
     ZSTD_cwksp_free(&cctx->workspace, cctx->customMem);
 }
 
